            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Lead with the Conclusion: Misaligned Numbers Are Normal; Aligned Ones Require Skill</h2>
<p>Many overseas teams encounter a perplexing phenomenon during financial reviews: $100,000 was deposited, $95,000 was spent, but the platform invoice shows only $88,000. This discrepancy isn't due to accounting errors—it's because overseas ad platforms naturally have "three layers of differences" in their billing mechanisms.</p>
<p>The first difference comes from <strong>the time gap between top-up and credit arrival</strong>. Major platforms like Meta, Google, and TikTok generally use a prepaid model, but funds typically take 1-3 business days to clear. During this period, if you've already started running campaigns, the balance shown in your account and the actual spendable amount will be temporarily out of sync. One tool app's advertising team once reported that during monthly reconciliation, they found $80,000 had been spent, but the account balance still showed over $10,000—because the funds deposited at month-end hadn't fully arrived yet.</p>
<p>The second difference is <strong>the inconsistency between spend measurement and invoice cycles</strong>. Platforms typically deduct spend in real-time, but invoices may be issued weekly, monthly, or even quarterly. Take Google Ads as an example: consumption data updates in real-time, but invoice amounts are generated based on the "accrued spend" statistics from the backend, which may differ from the figures in daily reports due to rounding at the decimal level. More complexly, some platforms list refunds and post-refund adjustments separately, causing the invoice total to not match the intuitive "total consumption."</p>
<p>The third difference, often overlooked, is <strong>exchange rate fluctuations and tax handling</strong>. Overseas ad platforms mostly settle in USD or EUR, but companies may need to convert to RMB for accounting. Exchange rates at the time of top-up, at the time of consumption recording, and at the time of invoicing can all be different. One cross-border e-commerce team conducted a review and found that if calculated at the top-up date exchange rate, they were at a loss, but at the consumption date exchange rate, they were profitable—this kind of "financial illusion" can cause teams to misjudge ROI.</p>
<h2>Three Steps to Double Reconciliation Efficiency</h2>
<p>Since discrepancies are inevitable, overseas teams need to systematize their reconciliation process rather than expecting the platform to provide a "perfectly aligned" number.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1: Establish a consumption-centered reconciliation logic</strong>. Don't use the top-up amount as the reconciliation benchmark. Instead, use the "accrued spend" from the platform backend as the standard. This number represents the actual consumption as recognized by the platform, closest to "services have been provided" in financial terms. Both the top-up balance and invoice amount should be compared against this benchmark, not against each other.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2: Distinguish between platform report and financial ledger data scopes</strong>. It's recommended to establish two sets of data tracking mechanisms internally: one for operational perspective consumption reports (focusing on delivery efficiency) and one for financial perspective billing reconciliation (focusing on fund safety). Both perspectives use the same data source, but the reconciliation timing and granularity can differ. Operational reports can be precise to the Campaign level, while financial reconciliation only needs to be precise to the account level.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3: Reserve a 3%-5% reconciliation tolerance</strong>. Based on the operational experience of multiple overseas teams, differences among top-up, consumption, and invoice are usually within 3%, which is considered normal. Only when this threshold is exceeded do you need to investigate whether there are duplicate charges, abnormal refunds, or platform system errors.</p>
<h2>Bottom Line</h2>
<p>The core of overseas ad reconciliation isn't "making numbers match" but <strong>understanding what each number represents</strong> and establishing a reconciliation system that can accommodate reasonable differences.</p>
